      "side": 1,
      "message": "But how do we ensure that this is seekable?\n\nThis seems pretty fragile; we\u0027re relying on a couple of assumptions:\n\n - that the underlying imageFd is seekable in the first place\n - that the `tar` process\u0027 `/proc/self/fd/N` aligns with the current process` fd (ie, that close on exec is not set!)\n \nIf you can guarantee the first, then you don\u0027t need the `/proc/self/fd/` facility in the first place, but just dup imageFd to tar\u0027s own stdin. `/proc/pid/fd/N` does not give you any special seek semantics, you may as well just pass the file descriptor directly.\n\nI\u0027m not too familiar with what imageFd is (or is not) guaranteed to be. If it\u0027s a regular file that the caller would have opened (ie, we can assume it is seekable), then I would suggest just setting tar\u0027s stdin directly, rather than popen().",
