      "writtenOn": "2026-05-25T22:33:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch — you\u0027re right on both counts. imageFd is guaranteed seekable because bmcweb writes the HTTP upload body to a temp file before calling StartUpdate, so the fd is always backed by a regular file. And your suggestion to dup it directly to tar\u0027s stdin is the cleaner fix.\n\nI\u0027ve fixed the code accordingly in patch set #4. Instead of /proc/self/fd/N via popen(), it now uses fork() + dup2(imageFd, STDIN_FILENO) + execl():\n\n```\n  pid_t pid \u003d fork();\n  if (pid \u003d\u003d 0)\n  {\n      dup2(imageFd, STDIN_FILENO);\n      execl(\"/bin/sh\", \"sh\", \"-c\", tarCmd.c_str(), nullptr);\n      _exit(127);\n  }\n```\n\nThis eliminates both concerns at once: no /proc/self/fd/ dependency, and the close-on-exec issue disappears because dup2() is called in the child before execl() — by POSIX, a fd produced by dup2() never inherits O_CLOEXEC from the source, so STDIN_FILENO always survives the exec. Since tar\u0027s stdin is now the seekable temp-file fd directly, BusyBox tar\u0027s built-in autodetect works without any magic-byte sniffing on our side with this fix.",
      "parentUuid": "8d06e774_183a4860",
